// **an owning value printed straight out of a call.** every other by-value argument hands the value to
// a callee, and a callee releases its parameter - `echo` has no callee at all, it is recognised at the
// call site and lowered inline, so the string here was read, printed, and then referred to by nothing.
// one leak per statement, and in a loop that is one per iteration.
//
// the leak is invisible in the output, which is the whole reason this is a golden: `string` declares no
// destructor and its buffer's teardown says nothing. the RAST is where the fix is visible - the print is
// wrapped in a binding that owns what it printed and releases it after the write

function label() : string
{
    string $s = 'buffer';
    $s->append('!');

    return $s;
}

echo 1;
echo label();
echo 2;

// a place is somebody else's already: a named local owns this one, so nothing is bound around the print
string $named = label();
echo $named;

// a literal binds too, and deliberately so: it is the same value a declaration initialized from one
// gets, and that declaration is released at the end of its scope. asking "does this expression own what
// it produced" any other way here would be a second answer to a question `needs_destruction` already
// owns - and the release is the same null-tolerant one, so a literal's static buffer is left alone
echo 'plain';
